const String _rawOsce = r'''01|Cardiology|Chest pain focused history|History|A 56-year-old man has central tightness on walking for 40 minutes.|Take focused history and identify acute coronary syndrome red flags.|Characterise pain and radiation; Ask associated symptoms; Establish cardiac risks; Review medicines and family history; Summarise and escalate|Do not delay emergency ECG and assessment for possible ACS.|Use SOCRATES and state the need for urgent ECG and senior review.
02|Respiratory|Worsening COPD breathlessness|History|A 68-year-old with COPD has worsening dyspnoea and increased sputum for three days.|Assess exacerbation severity and alternative diagnoses.|Clarify baseline and change; Ask sputum volume and colour; Screen fever and chest pain; Review inhalers and admissions; Identify respiratory distress|Exhaustion, silent chest or altered consciousness requires emergency escalation.|Consider pneumonia, heart failure and pulmonary embolism as well as COPD exacerbation.
03|Gastroenterology|Migratory right iliac fossa pain|History|A 24-year-old has pain migrating from the umbilicus to the right lower quadrant.|Take an acute abdominal pain history.|Characterise migration and severity; Ask nausea and appetite; Explore bowel and urinary symptoms; Establish pregnancy possibility; Identify peritonism and fever|Exclude ectopic pregnancy when pregnancy is possible.|Appendicitis is possible but the differential remains broad; assess urgently.
04|Neurology|Resolved focal weakness|History|A 72-year-old had right arm weakness and dysphasia for 20 minutes, now resolved.|Assess suspected TIA and establish exact timing.|Establish last-known-well; Characterise focal deficits; Ask duration and recurrence; Review AF and vascular risks; Check anticoagulants|Resolved symptoms still require urgent TIA assessment.|Do not reassure because symptoms resolved; activate urgent stroke/TIA pathway.
05|Endocrinology|Polyuria and polydipsia|History|A 19-year-old reports thirst, frequent urination and weight loss.|Assess new diabetes and screen for ketoacidosis.|Clarify thirst and nocturia; Ask weight loss and fatigue; Screen vomiting and abdominal pain; Review family history; Request glucose and ketones urgently|Vomiting, Kussmaul breathing or confusion may indicate DKA.|Rapid onset in a young person suggests possible type 1 diabetes; do not delay testing.
06|Psychiatry|Depression and suicide risk|History|A 32-year-old describes hopelessness and loss of enjoyment for two months.|Assess depressive symptoms and suicide risk sensitively.|Establish mood and anhedonia; Explore sleep and function; Ask directly about self-harm; Assess intent, plan and means; Identify supports and safety plan|Imminent intent or a specific plan requires urgent mental-health intervention.|Ask directly without judgement; explain limits of confidentiality when safety is at risk.
07|Obstetrics|Early pregnancy bleeding|History|A 27-year-old at seven weeks has spotting and lower abdominal discomfort.|Assess early pregnancy bleeding and ectopic risk.|Confirm gestation; Characterise bleeding and pain; Ask shoulder-tip pain and collapse; Review ectopic risk factors; Establish obstetric history|Instability, unilateral pain or collapse may indicate ruptured ectopic pregnancy.|Do not assume miscarriage before excluding ectopic pregnancy.
08|Paediatrics|Fever in an infant|History|A 10-month-old has fever, reduced feeding and fewer wet nappies.|Assess fever, hydration and serious illness signs.|Establish temperature and duration; Assess responsiveness; Ask feeding and wet nappies; Screen rash and breathing; Review immunisation and contacts|Poor responsiveness, non-blanching rash or respiratory distress needs urgent escalation.|Use parent observations and hydration markers; assess the child face to face.
09|Renal|Painless visible haematuria|History|A 61-year-old smoker has noticed red urine twice this week.|Explore haematuria and malignancy red flags.|Clarify blood and clots; Ask urinary symptoms and flank pain; Explore weight loss; Review smoking and exposures; Check anticoagulants and prior urology|Visible haematuria needs investigation even if it resolves or anticoagulants are used.|Do not attribute blood to anticoagulation without investigating.
10|Rheumatology|Inflammatory small-joint pain|History|A 39-year-old has symmetrical hand swelling and 90 minutes of morning stiffness.|Assess inflammatory arthritis and functional impact.|Map joint distribution; Establish morning stiffness; Ask systemic symptoms; Assess function; Review family history and medicines|A hot joint with fever may be septic arthritis and needs urgent review.|Prolonged morning stiffness and synovitis warrant early specialist assessment.
11|Cardiology|Cardiovascular examination|Examination|A 64-year-old with exertional dyspnoea is referred for examination.|Perform and present a structured cardiovascular examination.|Inspect hands and face; Assess pulse and blood pressure; Measure JVP; Palpate apex and precordium; Auscultate valves and assess oedema|Recognise acute heart failure and escalate respiratory distress.|Include peripheral signs, JVP, precordium, heart sounds and congestion.
12|Respiratory|Respiratory examination|Examination|A 58-year-old with chronic cough attends clinic.|Perform a systematic respiratory examination.|Inspect respiratory effort; Examine hands and face; Assess trachea and expansion; Percuss both lungs; Auscultate apices to bases|Severe hypoxia or distress requires immediate escalation.|Compare sides systematically and correlate percussion with auscultation.
13|Neurology|Upper limb neurological examination|Examination|A patient reports progressive right hand weakness and tingling.|Perform a focused upper limb neurological examination.|Inspect wasting and tremor; Assess tone; Test power and grade; Assess reflexes; Map sensation and coordination|Rapidly progressive weakness or bulbar symptoms need urgent escalation.|Compare sides and distinguish upper from lower motor neuron patterns.
14|Neurology|Cranial nerve examination|Examination|A patient presents with facial asymmetry and altered vision.|Perform a structured cranial nerve examination.|Assess visual acuity and fields; Examine pupils and eye movements; Test facial sensation; Assess facial movement and hearing; Examine palate, tongue and shoulder shrug|Acute focal cranial nerve deficits may represent stroke.|Careful eye movement assessment helps anatomical localisation.
15|Gastroenterology|Abdominal examination|Examination|A 52-year-old with chronic liver disease has abdominal distension.|Perform a complete abdominal examination.|Inspect abdomen and stigmata; Palpate all quadrants; Assess liver and spleen; Percuss liver span and ascites; Auscultate and assess oedema|Guarding, encephalopathy or GI bleeding requires urgent escalation.|Look for ascites and portal hypertension alongside chronic liver disease signs.
16|Endocrinology|Thyroid examination|Examination|A 35-year-old has palpitations, weight loss and neck swelling.|Perform a focused thyroid examination.|Inspect neck and eyes; Observe swallowing; Palpate thyroid and nodes; Assess retrosternal extension; Auscultate bruit and check tremor|Stridor or rapidly enlarging swelling requires urgent assessment.|A thyroid swelling usually moves on swallowing; assess systemic signs.
17|Musculoskeletal|Knee examination|Examination|A 47-year-old has knee pain on stairs and prolonged walking.|Perform a structured comparative knee examination.|Inspect alignment and gait; Palpate joint line and patella; Assess effusion; Test range of movement; Perform appropriate stability tests|A hot swollen knee with fever may be septic arthritis.|Use look-feel-move and avoid painful provocative tests in acute injury.
18|Musculoskeletal|Shoulder examination|Examination|A 43-year-old has overhead shoulder pain and night discomfort.|Perform a focused shoulder examination.|Inspect symmetry and wasting; Palpate landmarks; Assess active movement; Assess passive movement; Test rotator cuff and impingement|Traumatic deformity or neurovascular compromise needs urgent assessment.|Restricted passive movement suggests pathology beyond isolated rotator cuff pain.
19|Obstetrics|Antenatal abdominal examination|Examination|A 30-week pregnant patient attends routine review with normal fetal movements.|Demonstrate focused antenatal examination.|Confirm gestation and consent; Inspect abdomen; Measure fundal height; Perform Leopold manoeuvres; Locate fetal heart and explain findings|Reduced movements, bleeding or fluid loss needs prompt obstetric assessment.|Use gentle technique and interpret fundal height in gestational context.
20|Paediatrics|Developmental assessment|Examination|A 2-year-old is reviewed for limited speech.|Take developmental overview and observe age-appropriate skills.|Explore parental concern; Assess gross motor; Assess fine motor; Explore speech and hearing; Assess social play and regression|Loss of acquired skills requires prompt assessment.|Use a broad developmental framework; one milestone alone is not diagnostic.
21|Communication|Explain type 2 diabetes|Communication|A 50-year-old is worried after blood tests suggest type 2 diabetes.|Explain diagnosis and agree initial next steps.|Explore prior understanding; Explain in plain language; Respond to emotions; Discuss lifestyle without blame; Use teach-back and agree follow-up|Do not imply complications are inevitable or promise reversal.|Use shared decisions and check understanding.
22|Communication|Breaking bad news|Communication|Imaging shows a suspicious lung mass; histology is pending.|Communicate the result compassionately and accurately.|Ensure privacy; Explore what patient knows; Give warning shot; Explain result and uncertainty; Respond to emotion and plan next steps|Do not call it confirmed cancer before tissue diagnosis.|Be honest about uncertainty and allow time for questions.
23|Communication|Consent for lumbar puncture|Communication|A patient with suspected meningitis is considered for lumbar puncture.|Explain purpose, benefits, risks and alternatives.|Assess capacity; Explain purpose and samples; Describe steps and discomfort; Discuss material risks; Check understanding and document choice|Assess contraindications such as suspected raised intracranial pressure before proceeding.|Consent is a dialogue and must be voluntary.
