25|Communication|Warfarin counselling|Communication|A 70-year-old with atrial fibrillation is starting warfarin and fears bleeding.|Explain INR monitoring, interactions and precautions.|Explain indication; Describe INR checks; Discuss consistent vitamin K intake; Warn about interactions; Explain bleeding red flags and missed doses|Head injury or major bleeding while anticoagulated needs urgent assessment.|Emphasise monitoring and checking before new medicines.
26|Communication|Smoking cessation|Communication|A 45-year-old smoker is not ready to quit because smoking relieves stress.|Use motivational interviewing to explore readiness.|Ask permission; Explore perceived benefits; Assess dependence and previous attempts; Reflect and link to personal goals; Offer support and agree next step|Do not shame or withdraw care if the patient is not ready.|Respect autonomy; brief advice can build motivation.
27|Communication|DNACPR discussion|Communication|An 84-year-old asks what happens if their heart stops during admission.|Discuss CPR and escalation planning sensitively.|Explore values; Explain CPR plainly; Discuss likely benefits and burdens; Clarify DNACPR scope; Check understanding and document|DNACPR does not mean no treatment.|Individualise discussion and distinguish CPR from wider treatment plans.
28|Communication|Contraception counselling|Communication|A 22-year-old wants contraception but is unsure which method suits her.|Provide balanced counselling and support informed choice.|Explore preferences; Review medical history and medicines; Explain LARC; Discuss hormonal options and risks; Assess STI risk and shared choice|Screen contraindications before recommending oestrogen-containing contraception.|Offer options without coercion and respect confidentiality.
29|Communication|Reactive HIV screening result|Communication|A 29-year-old has a preliminary reactive HIV screening result and is distressed.|Explain screening, confirmatory testing and support without stigma.|Ensure privacy; Explain screening versus confirmation; Respond empathetically; Discuss confidentiality; Arrange confirmation and linkage to care|A reactive screen alone is not a confirmed diagnosis.|Use neutral language and avoid assumptions about transmission.
30|Emergency|ABCDE assessment|Emergency|A 59-year-old is confused, pale, clammy and tachypnoeic in ED.|Demonstrate immediate ABCDE assessment and escalation.|Call for help; Assess airway; Assess breathing and oxygenation; Assess circulation and bleeding; Assess disability, glucose and exposure|Treat life threats as found; do not delay for detailed history.|ABCDE is dynamic; intervene and reassess.
31|Emergency|Adult basic life support|Emergency|A 55-year-old collapses and is unresponsive in a clinic.|Demonstrate initial response to suspected cardiac arrest.|Check safety and response; Activate emergency response; Assess breathing briefly; Start high-quality compressions; Apply AED and follow prompts|Agonal gasps are not normal breathing; minimise delay to compressions.|Follow current resuscitation guidance and team roles.
32|Emergency|Anaphylaxis first response|Emergency|A patient develops wheeze, facial swelling and hypotension after an antibiotic.|Recognise anaphylaxis and outline first-line management.|Stop trigger and call help; Position appropriately; Give IM adrenaline per protocol; Provide oxygen and monitoring; Establish access and reassess|IM adrenaline is first-line; antihistamines must not delay it.|Use correct route and site and reassess ABC repeatedly.
33|Emergency|Acute severe asthma|Emergency|A 21-year-old with asthma cannot complete sentences and is distressed.|Assess severity and outline immediate treatment.|Recognise severe features; Assess observations and peak flow if safe; Call senior help; Give oxygen and bronchodilator per protocol; Escalate poor response|Silent chest, exhaustion or confusion is life-threatening.|Do not delay treatment for peak flow in a very unwell patient.
34|Emergency|Conscious hypoglycaemia|Emergency|A patient with diabetes is sweaty; glucose is 2.8 mmol/L and they can swallow.|Treat conscious hypoglycaemia and recheck.|Confirm symptoms and glucose; Assess swallow safety; Give fast oral glucose; Recheck at protocol interval; Give longer-acting carbohydrate and identify cause|Never give oral intake if consciousness or swallow is impaired.|Treat, recheck and address the cause.
